.Dd October 31, 2026
.Dt MOUNT_XFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount_xfs
.Nd experimental read-only XFS filesystem in user space
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl o Cm ro
.Ar image mountpoint
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
serves a quiescent XFS regular image file through
.Xr puffs 4
and
.Xr puffs 3 .
The XFS reader runs in the server process.
The journal must contain a complete clean unmount record;
dirty, ambiguous, malformed and unsupported journals are rejected.
There is no journal replay or write support.
.Pp
The mount is always read-only, nodev, nosuid and noatime.
The only accepted mount option is
.Cm ro .
The
.Fl s
option keeps the server in the foreground.
Otherwise it detaches using
.Fn puffs_daemon .
.Pp
Regular files, directories and symbolic links are supported.
UIDs, GIDs and mode bits are preserved, and directory search and access
checks use the credentials supplied by PUFFS.
Inodes with attribute forks are rejected because ACL and security attribute
interpretation is not implemented.
Special files, external logs and realtime devices are unsupported.
.Pp
The backing file is opened read-only and held under an advisory shared lock.
It must remain unchanged for the entire mount.
A detected size, modification-time or change-time change causes subsequent
operations to fail with
.Er ESTALE .
This detection and the advisory lock do not provide snapshot isolation.
.Pp
The initial server uses synchronous reads and disables PUFFS name, attribute
and page caches.
Memory mapping and execution of files from this mount are unsupported.
Directory listing and lookup scan directory data linearly.
Directory offsets are stable entry ordinals on the immutable image.
External directory indexes and global allocation consistency are not verified.
.Pp
Use
.Xr umount 8
to unmount.
SIGINT and SIGTERM request an unmount through libpuffs.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount_xfs -s -o ro /var/tmp/test.xfs /mnt/xfs
ls /mnt/xfs
umount /mnt/xfs
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr puffs 3 ,
.Xr puffs 4 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr umount 8
.Sh HISTORY
This experimental GPL port uses SGI/Linux XFS sources pinned to Linux v6.12.
It is built explicitly from external/gpl2/xfs/puffs and is not part of the
default base-system build.
